<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=wheelbarrow">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wheelbarrow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">118</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A wheelbarrow is a small hand-propelled vehicle, usually with one wheel, pushed and guided by a single person using two handles at the rear. The earliest evidence comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>: a painted tomb mural at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, dated to 118 CE, shows a man pushing one. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Records of the Three Kingdoms</a> credit the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a> with a 'wooden ox' that carried military supplies in 231 CE. Chinese wheelbarrows mostly carried the load over a large central wheel, while those that appeared in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> between 1170 and 1250 had the wheel at or near the front.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wheelbarrow.jpg" alt="Wheelbarrow" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ildar Sagdejev (Specious) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2008-07-15_Construction_wheelbarrow_at_Duke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The wheelbarrow is designed to share the weight of its load between the wheel and the person pushing it, so that heavier and bulkier loads can be carried than a person could carry alone. A stone relief in the tomb of Shen Fujun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, dated to about 150 CE, shows a man pushing one, and a mural in the Wu Liang tomb-shrine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, dated to 147 CE, depicts the story of the dutiful Dong Yuan pushing his father in a single-wheeled barrow. The commentator Pei Songzhi described the wooden ox of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a> as a large single central wheel and axle with a wooden frame built around it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Chinese barrows came in two kinds. Earlier ones were front-wheeled, while the more common type after the third century had a large central wheel and could carry up to six passengers. In 1797 Andreas Everardus van Braam Houckgeest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> described them accurately, and also reported near the southern border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> a fleet of larger barrows fitted with matting or cloth sails.</p><p>The question of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> is open. M. J. T. Lewis argued that a temple inventory from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Eleusis</a> of 408/407 and 407/406 BCE, which lists a body for a one-wheeler, points to a wheelbarrow on Greek building sites, but he admitted that this was speculation, and there is no secure evidence for the wheelbarrow in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Research on the early European barrow is hard because there was no common word for it. Lewis found four mentions in English and French sources between 1172 and 1222, three of them under different terms. The first archival reference, according to Andrea Matthies, dates from 1222 and records the purchase of several for the English king's works at Dover, and the first picture is in Matthew Paris's Vitae duorum Offarum, completed in 1250. By the 13th century the barrow served in building, mining and farming, but it stayed rare until the 15th century and seems to have been confined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wheelbarrow” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>118 CE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Chengdu, Sichuan, China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The Records of the Three Kingdoms credit Zhuge Liang with its invention in 231 CE; M. J. T. Lewis read a 'one-wheeler' in temple inventories at Eleusis of 408–406 BCE as a Greek wheelbarrow, but there is no evidence for it in Greece or Rome.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/wheel">Wheel<span>before 4000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=wheelbarrow">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
